{
  "requirement": "The `Python` implementation of `OutlineLang` supplies the extension, selection, and fingerprint rules described in this chapter.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct Python;\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for Python;\nimpl Debug for Python;\nimpl Default for Python;\nimpl OutlineLang for Python;\nimpl Copy for Python;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.sources.python.Python` has two obligations of its own. First, the declaration: `pub struct Python;` with `Clone`, `Debug`, `Default`, `OutlineLang` and `Copy` implementations. Second, that the type supplies the extension, selection and fingerprint rules described in the chapter. The detailed rules belong to refining requirements (`python.language`, `python.fingerprints`, `python.items`), which are assessed on their own, so they need no test here.\n\n- **Declaration and trait implementations.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` compares each declaration in the SDK book chapters with the generated rustdoc. Its helper `check_declaration` asserts equality of the expected trait-implementation set against `RustdocSignatures::trait_impls`, and the same for member names. A missing or extra `Clone`, `Debug`, `Default`, `Copy` or `OutlineLang` implementation, or a changed struct shape, would fail it. I did not read this test's handling of the `Python` page directly, so I am assuming it covers every declaration in the chapters. That assumption rests on `check_declaration`'s generic design, which is a small residual uncertainty.\n- **Supplies the extension and fingerprint rules.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` constructs `languages::Python` through the public API. It checks the extensions `py` and `pyi` (with an uppercase `.PYI` file name), the `python-structural-v1` fingerprint, and the outline selector `def[check]`.\n- **Selection rules.** `python_selected_source_includes_its_decorators` exercises Python selection through `OutlineContent`. It checks that decorators are included for functions and for duplicate-named classes (`#1` and `#2`).\n\nI found no unexercised obligation of the subject itself.",
